<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-sampling-means> {
  ex:l5-c13a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:sampling-distribution-of-mean ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-c13a-heading, ex:l5-c13a-plot, ex:l5-c13a-math ; cd:narration "Now make the environmental mean estimator the main object. For this hypothetical investigation, the target is the volume-weighted mean in a defined water body at one fixed survey time. Idealize selection as independent equal-chance draws of equal-volume units; this is a teaching sampling model, not a validated field design. Unequal selection probabilities, stratification or spatial dependence would require corresponding weights and uncertainty calculations. To examine an estimator against known truth, use a new synthetic lognormal population with mean 10 mg/L and SD 9 mg/L. These are simulation benchmarks, not the parameters of the preceding L12 illustration and not estimates from field data. In a real investigation the mean is unknown. The curve describes individual water-unit concentrations; its right tail extends beyond the 0 to 80 mg/L drawing. Assume the analytical contribution is negligible for this isolated sampling demonstration, not for real monitoring. Next take one sample, estimate its mean, and then examine how much that estimator varies across simulated investigations."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-c13b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:sampling-distribution-of-mean ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-c13b-heading, ex:l5-c13b-plot, ex:l5-c13b-math ; cd:narration "Our one simulated investigation selects four water units under the stated idealized equal-chance, equal-volume model. Their concentrations are about 3.677657, 10.308606, 2.394771 and 4.344606 mg/L. They are separate simulated environmental units, not laboratory repeats of one sample. Add the unrounded values and divide by four to obtain 5.181410018887113 mg/L, or 5.18. Under this selection model, the arithmetic sample mean estimates the water-body mean. The mean marker is a summary, not a fifth individual observation. The true benchmark 10 mg/L is available only because this is a simulation; it is not an input to the sample-mean calculation. The difference shows that an unbiased sampling method does not force every realized estimate to equal the truth. An actual report must accompany the estimate with design, scope and an uncertainty assessment. This detail plot has its own labelled axis; surrounding distribution comparisons use 0 to 80 mg/L. Keep this first sample once in the next set of five thousand simulated investigations to study the estimator's variability."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-c13c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:sampling-distribution-of-mean ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-c13c-heading, ex:l5-c13c-text, ex:l5-c13c-plot ; cd:narration "Why can our estimate of 5.18 mg/L differ from the water-body mean despite the stipulated selection model. Examine five thousand simulated investigations, each with four fresh independent water-unit draws and one mean. This thought experiment reveals sampling variation; it does not require five thousand real surveys before reporting one estimate. All twenty thousand individual draws contribute to their respective means; they are not pooled as if they were five thousand means. The original population remains unchanged. Its theoretical density and the simulated sampling histogram share 0 to 80 mg/L and density units L/mg. All five thousand means are retained, with maximum about 44.52 mg/L; no simulated means fall outside the display. Published bin counts preserve the frequencies without reconstructing unknown within-bin values. The average of means is about 9.99968 mg/L and their descriptive SD about 4.42932 mg/L, not forced theoretical equalities. This SD characterizes estimator variation under the simulation, not variation of individual concentrations or full measurement uncertainty. The means remain right-skewed: size four does not establish a normal distribution. Next vary sample size to explain how a justified larger environmental sample can improve precision without repairing a biased design. These are pseudorandom model results, not physical field evidence."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
